Step-by-Step Audio Guided Tour: Picasso, Miro, Dali: Unbound exhibition at MFA, Boston, MA - Museum Tour
Tour-Category: museum

Stop 1: Le Lézard aux plumes d’or (The Lizard with Golden Feathers)

Address: 465 Huntington Ave, Boston, MA 02115

Coordinates: 42.339444, -71.094167

Orientation: You are about to explore the Picasso, Miro, Dali: Unbound exhibition at the Museum of Fine Arts in Boston, Massachusetts. Within the Lois B. and Michael K. Torf Gallery, this extraordinary collection of Spanish artists' works is a testament to the transformative power of the livre d'artiste, or artist's book. These deeply collaborative ventures revolutionized the book as an art form, offering a rare view into how images, words, and typography intersect. Amidst the exhibition, you will encounter three remarkable works: "Le Lézard aux plumes d’or" (The Lizard with Golden Feathers), "Moses and Monotheism," and "Au Soleil du Plafond." The exhibition highlights the transformative power of belief and imagination in art and human history, presenting creations that had no precedent. Each piece stands as a testament to the visionary spirit of Picasso, Miro, and Dali, challenging the confines of traditional bookmaking to elevate it into an extraordinary art form. In the stops ahead, you will encounter how Dalí's visual interpretations and Freud's provocative prose transform the book into a singular, unified artwork at Moses and Monotheism, and Tériade's decision to bring Juan Gris and Pierre Reverdy together exemplified his commitment to fostering collaboration at Au Soleil du Plafond. Your first stop is Le Lézard aux plumes d’or (The Lizard with Golden Feathers). Stand close enough to Le Lézard aux plumes d’or (The Lizard with Golden Feathers) to see the texture of the Louis Broder’s vellum, allowing the depth of the color lithographs to emerge, revealing the intricate interplay of image and text that characterizes this surrealist masterpiece.

Le Lézard aux plumes d’or, created by Joan Miró in 1971, vividly explores surrealist themes through an illustrated book containing 40 color lithographs. Recognizing its significance, generously donated this work to the Museum of Fine Arts, Boston, enhancing the museum's collection of surrealist-era printed works. This piece exemplifies the exhibition's argument that such collaborations were revolutionary, challenging the boundaries between text and image and examining the role of myth in shaping our perception of reality. The broader cultural context it inhabits shows where myths and surrealism intersect to challenge and expand our understanding of the world.


Directions: Continue through Museum of Fine Arts, Boston — next is Moses and Monotheism.



Stop 2: Moses and Monotheism

Address: 465 Huntington Ave, Boston, MA 02115

Coordinates: 42.339444, -71.094167

Orientation: In the gallery named for patron Torf, the exhibit "Moses and Monotheism" unfolds a narrative where art and psychoanalysis converge.

In 1974, Salvador Dalí, the prominent spanish surrealist painter, illustrated Sigmund Freud's seminal work, "Moses and Monotheism," a text published by The Hogarth Press. Freud, the father of psychoanalysis, explored the deep psychological undercurrents of religious belief, positing that Moses himself was an Egyptian and a follower of Akhenaten, who introduced monotheism. Dalí, known for his surreal imagery, brought these complex ideas to life through his illustrations. The interplay of Dalí's visual interpretations and Freud's provocative prose transforms the book into a singular, unified artwork. The Hogarth Press's decision to publish this edition underscored the importance of such collaborations in advancing the form, marrying the intellectual rigor of Freud with Dalí's evocative illustrations. These editions, normally kept in archives, are rare glimpses into the transformative power of artistic partnerships. Through Dalí's lens, the evolution of monotheism mirrors the evolution of modern art, both deeply intertwined with the complexities of human thought and cultural change.


Directions: Your final stop in Museum of Fine Arts, Boston: Au Soleil du Plafond.



Stop 3: Au Soleil du Plafond

Address: 465 Huntington Ave, Boston, MA 02115

Coordinates: 42.339444, -71.094167

Orientation: This work is part of an exhibition centered on the profound impact such collaborations had on the evolution of the art book.

In 1955, Tériade, a prominent publisher known for his dedication to the livre d'artiste, published Au Soleil du Plafond, a project that had been envisioned decades earlier. Tériade's decision to bring Juan Gris, a leading figure of the Cubist movement, and Pierre Reverdy together for this project exemplified his commitment to fostering collaboration between visual artists and poets. This collaboration resulted in a book that integrates eleven lithographs by Gris with Reverdy's manuscript lithographed text, showcasing an intricate dialogue between image and word.



That's 3 stops — Le Lézard aux plumes d’or exemplifies revolutionary collaborations challenging text and image boundaries and Moses and Monotheism transforms into a singular artwork through Dalí and Freud's interplay. Closing: The Treat Page shows whether there are real savings at local shops and restaurants around here. We can also generate news articles for you to listen to on the way back.

